1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,570
Hi everyone, and welcome
to Conf42 Chaos Engineering 2026.

2
00:00:03,839 --> 00:00:07,672
I'm Ajay Munjuluru, a senior lead
software engineer at JPMorgan Chase.

3
00:00:08,531 --> 00:00:10,945
Most of my career has
been in enterprise CRM.

4
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:15,586
A lot of Salesforce and a lot of
time spent on one question: Who

5
00:00:15,586 --> 00:00:17,538
is allowed to see which record?

6
00:00:18,068 --> 00:00:21,940
Sharing models, field se-- field-level
security, identity, and access.

7
00:00:22,675 --> 00:00:26,181
For the last couple of years, I've
been building multi-agent systems

8
00:00:26,181 --> 00:00:29,695
on top of the same kind of data,
and this talk is really about what

9
00:00:29,703 --> 00:00:31,648
happens when those two worlds meet.

10
00:00:32,405 --> 00:00:36,884
One quick note before I start, everything
on these slides is made up for this talk.

11
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:39,688
There's no client data, nothing
from my employer's systems, and

12
00:00:39,688 --> 00:00:40,972
the opinions are completely mine.

13
00:00:41,972 --> 00:00:43,119
What we usually test.

14
00:00:43,439 --> 00:00:45,603
So let me start with what
most of us test today.

15
00:00:46,679 --> 00:00:51,843
When I look at agent eval suites,
whether it's our own, a vendor's, or

16
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:55,566
one of the open source frameworks,
they're mostly about the answer.

17
00:00:56,323 --> 00:00:57,129
Is it accurate?

18
00:00:57,405 --> 00:00:58,249
Was it helpful?

19
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:00,403
Did the agent finish the task?

20
00:01:00,972 --> 00:01:04,330
Did the tool calls go through,
and what did the whole thing cost?

21
00:01:05,026 --> 00:01:09,211
All of that is useful, but none of
those checks care who is asking.

22
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:13,278
Who can have an agent write a perfect
summary of a client relationship

23
00:01:13,735 --> 00:01:16,517
for someone who isn't supposed
to know half of what's in it?

24
00:01:17,483 --> 00:01:19,728
And every one of those
metrics will come back green.

25
00:01:20,541 --> 00:01:21,933
This isn't a new risk.

26
00:01:22,934 --> 00:01:28,272
OWASP's top ten LLM application lists
sensitive information disclosure.

27
00:01:29,509 --> 00:01:34,077
What I don't see very often is teams
actually testing for it on every release.

28
00:01:35,055 --> 00:01:37,288
It tends to live in a threat
model document somewhere.

29
00:01:38,046 --> 00:01:43,951
I think it belongs in the test suite
next to everything else on the left, and

30
00:01:43,951 --> 00:01:47,610
I think it matters more now than it did
a year ago, which brings me to the next

31
00:01:47,610 --> 00:01:51,364
slide One front door, many sets of rules.

32
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:54,370
Here's how I think about what changed.

33
00:01:55,188 --> 00:01:58,562
Users used to go each, to
each system on its own.

34
00:01:59,437 --> 00:02:04,454
The CRM, the data warehouse, wherever
the document lives, their email.

35
00:02:05,405 --> 00:02:08,278
Each of those had its own
front door and its own rules.

36
00:02:08,687 --> 00:02:12,771
In CRM terms, that's profiles,
permission sets, field level security,

37
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:14,738
sharing rules, the role hierarchy.

38
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:17,603
The warehouse has role level policies.

39
00:02:17,989 --> 00:02:19,622
Documents have ACLs.

40
00:02:20,485 --> 00:02:25,836
Now, people want to ask one question
in one place, and the agent goes

41
00:02:25,836 --> 00:02:27,170
and gets the answer from everywhere.

42
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:32,675
So the agent ends up doing something
none of those systems ever had to do.

43
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:38,514
It has to combine all of those rule sets
on the fly for every request, and then

44
00:02:38,514 --> 00:02:40,204
describe the result in plain language.

45
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:44,898
I wrote a paper on agentic AI over
enterprise CRM earlier this year,

46
00:02:44,944 --> 00:02:46,362
and this is its main argument.

47
00:02:47,357 --> 00:02:51,093
In regulated setting, combining
entitlements across systems

48
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:52,411
is what really limits us.

49
00:02:53,287 --> 00:02:55,639
It's less about how
well the model reasons.

50
00:02:55,978 --> 00:03:00,144
If anything, a better model makes
this a little harder because it

51
00:03:00,163 --> 00:03:04,152
gets better at putting two and two
together Now moving on to my next

52
00:03:04,153 --> 00:03:07,130
slide where leaks actually show up.

53
00:03:07,732 --> 00:03:09,148
So where do leaks actually show up?

54
00:03:09,479 --> 00:03:11,297
I would group them into
five places, right?

55
00:03:11,321 --> 00:03:15,664
The response is obvious one, and honestly,
it's the only one most suites check.

56
00:03:16,601 --> 00:03:18,095
Then summaries and memory.

57
00:03:19,037 --> 00:03:22,475
Agents write things down, a call
note gets logged back to the

58
00:03:22,478 --> 00:03:27,076
sy- CRM, a summary gets cached,
something goes into a memory store.

59
00:03:27,868 --> 00:03:31,682
If that note lands on a record that
more people can see than the sources

60
00:03:31,682 --> 00:03:35,525
it came from, you have basically moved
restricted data into a shared place.

61
00:03:36,738 --> 00:03:39,045
Tools call can leak in two ways.

62
00:03:39,754 --> 00:03:44,210
The argument themselves, like a search
query that has a deal code name, in

63
00:03:44,210 --> 00:03:47,941
it and gets logged somewhere with
different access or over fetching,

64
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:52,379
where the agent pulls fifty fields
when it only needed five, and now the

65
00:03:52,379 --> 00:03:54,432
restricted ones are sitting in context.

66
00:03:55,733 --> 00:03:58,692
Context and retrieval is the
one I would worry about most.

67
00:03:59,533 --> 00:04:03,787
If retrieval brings back chunks the
user isn't entitled to, entitled to,

68
00:04:04,531 --> 00:04:09,122
the restricted facts are in the prompt
before the model writes a single word.

69
00:04:10,624 --> 00:04:14,204
So filter retrieval by the user's
entitlements before the context is built.

70
00:04:15,507 --> 00:04:19,781
The same thing happens in multi-agent
setups when a planner hands a sub-agent

71
00:04:19,858 --> 00:04:25,746
more than its task needs, and in a
long conversation that of all, of

72
00:04:25,767 --> 00:04:27,381
all that accumulates turn after turn.

73
00:04:28,428 --> 00:04:29,915
The last row is joins.

74
00:04:30,757 --> 00:04:34,713
Two facts the user is allowed to see
that add up to the one they aren't.

75
00:04:35,295 --> 00:04:40,516
I'll come back to that because it's the
hardest one to test The way I make this

76
00:04:40,612 --> 00:04:44,093
testable is to write down as a property.

77
00:04:44,682 --> 00:04:50,207
For each persona and each task, everything
the agent produces has to stay inside

78
00:04:50,219 --> 00:04:51,933
what the persona is allowed to see.

79
00:04:53,005 --> 00:04:56,201
And everything means the response,
any summaries it writes, the

80
00:04:56,212 --> 00:05:00,113
arguments it sends to tools, and
whatever it hands to other agents.

81
00:05:00,834 --> 00:05:04,106
Once you write it like that, you
can actually build a test for it.

82
00:05:04,531 --> 00:05:06,440
In practice, I need three things.

83
00:05:07,023 --> 00:05:12,666
First, a fixed test data and real test
users, so when something fails, I can

84
00:05:12,738 --> 00:05:14,541
run it again and get the same failure.

85
00:05:15,188 --> 00:05:19,012
Second, the expected results have
to come from the source system.

86
00:05:19,889 --> 00:05:23,907
I don't want the test author
deciding by hand who should see what.

87
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:27,644
The CRM already knows
that, and I ask the CRM.

88
00:05:28,521 --> 00:05:32,175
The third, the result has to be
able to block a res- a release.

89
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:36,274
if it can't, people will look
at it once and then stop.

90
00:05:37,662 --> 00:05:43,376
The next few slides go through each part:
personas, canaries, and the source of the

91
00:05:43,376 --> 00:05:48,040
truth check, the assertions, the joins,
when to run it, and how to debug a failure

92
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:50,528
So let's start with personas.

93
00:05:51,057 --> 00:05:52,036
The idea is simple.

94
00:05:52,657 --> 00:05:55,750
Run the same tasks at different
users with different access.

95
00:05:56,573 --> 00:06:00,543
If you only test as one user and
a lot of teams test as a developer

96
00:06:00,552 --> 00:06:04,130
with very broad access, you aren't
really testing authorization at all.

97
00:06:05,112 --> 00:06:07,096
Here's an example set for a banking CRM.

98
00:06:07,976 --> 00:06:11,283
A coverage banker who sees
their own book and their tea--

99
00:06:11,331 --> 00:06:12,931
and their teams in one region.

100
00:06:13,817 --> 00:06:18,601
Someone on a private side, deal team who's
been wall crossed on exactly one deal.

101
00:06:19,903 --> 00:06:23,220
A public side analyst who should
never see anything non-public.

102
00:06:23,814 --> 00:06:28,135
A service side user who works cases
but can't see pipeline or credit.

103
00:06:28,739 --> 00:06:33,227
A platform admin who has lots of
configuration access but no business

104
00:06:33,235 --> 00:06:34,887
reason to see client financials.

105
00:06:35,764 --> 00:06:37,177
People forget that one a lot.

106
00:06:37,706 --> 00:06:40,874
And a compliance reve--
reviewer who can read almost

107
00:06:40,968 --> 00:06:42,595
everything but should never act.

108
00:06:43,191 --> 00:06:45,328
That one checks the opposite direction.

109
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,993
Broad visibility shouldn't turn into
writes, emails or notifications.

110
00:06:50,775 --> 00:06:53,811
The right-hand column is, what
makes each persona useful.

111
00:06:54,558 --> 00:06:58,705
Those are the things they must
never see, and that column

112
00:06:58,714 --> 00:06:59,989
turns into your assertions.

113
00:07:01,139 --> 00:07:03,731
The most important part
is a line at the bottom.

114
00:07:04,724 --> 00:07:08,258
These need to be real test logins with
real permission sets, roles, and sharing.

115
00:07:09,181 --> 00:07:13,269
If the persona only exists in the prompt,
you're testing whether the model follows

116
00:07:13,271 --> 00:07:16,882
instructions, which is a different
question from whether your access model

117
00:07:16,897 --> 00:07:19,620
holds Moving on to the next slide.

118
00:07:20,595 --> 00:07:23,569
The next question I have is: how
do you know something leaked?

119
00:07:24,344 --> 00:07:25,602
I plant canaries.

120
00:07:25,774 --> 00:07:29,676
A canary is a fake restricted fact
that is unique and easy to spot.

121
00:07:30,222 --> 00:07:33,237
A deal codename like
Project, Halcyon, right?

122
00:07:33,277 --> 00:07:37,497
An inter-an exposure number with odd
precision, like forty-seven million

123
00:07:37,502 --> 00:07:41,154
three hundred and eighty thousand,
that won't show up by accident.

124
00:07:42,481 --> 00:07:44,465
A case flag with a made-up reference.

125
00:07:45,395 --> 00:07:48,442
And I put them everywhere the data
lives, on the opportunity record,

126
00:07:48,444 --> 00:07:52,581
in a free text note, inside a PDF
attachment, in an email thread.

127
00:07:52,982 --> 00:07:55,644
In my experience, the, the
leak usually doesn't come

128
00:07:55,645 --> 00:07:57,022
from a field you are watching.

129
00:07:57,127 --> 00:07:59,715
It comes from the, a
note someone pasted into.

130
00:08:00,923 --> 00:08:03,850
The other half is working out what
each person is supposed to see.

131
00:08:04,738 --> 00:08:06,249
I don't write that down by hand.

132
00:08:06,636 --> 00:08:10,490
I log in as each test user, query
the source system through the same

133
00:08:10,493 --> 00:08:14,998
API with their system's, with their
user's token, and save what comes back.

134
00:08:15,985 --> 00:08:20,090
The CRM sharing engine already knows
the answer, so I let it tell me.

135
00:08:20,983 --> 00:08:22,521
Those are two reasons I like this.

136
00:08:22,959 --> 00:08:26,400
Nobody can get it wrong by hand, and
when somebody changes a sharing rule

137
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:30,581
next quarter, the expected results
change automatically, and the suite

138
00:08:30,588 --> 00:08:31,867
tells me whether the agent kept up

139
00:08:32,867 --> 00:08:37,699
Once you have canaries and expected
sets, you can write the actual checks.

140
00:08:39,074 --> 00:08:40,260
I use three kinds.

141
00:08:40,622 --> 00:08:42,237
The first is exact match.

142
00:08:43,324 --> 00:08:47,895
Does any canaries string or, or
number show up in the respona--

143
00:08:47,912 --> 00:08:52,164
in, in the response, in a summary,
or in any tool call argument?

144
00:08:52,704 --> 00:08:55,526
It's cheap, and it doesn't
really produce false positives.

145
00:08:56,307 --> 00:08:58,101
So any hit blocks the release.

146
00:08:58,895 --> 00:09:02,573
The second is about meaning,
because models paraphrase.

147
00:09:03,224 --> 00:09:05,218
The agent won't say Project Halcyon.

148
00:09:05,554 --> 00:09:07,680
It'll s- it'll say something
like, "There's a confidential

149
00:09:07,681 --> 00:09:10,562
transaction in the works, somewhere
in the mid-forties of millions."

150
00:09:11,405 --> 00:09:15,617
So a judge model gets a list of
restricted facts for that persona

151
00:09:15,617 --> 00:09:17,288
and answers one narrow question.

152
00:09:18,211 --> 00:09:20,604
Does this output reveal
or hint at any of them?

153
00:09:21,118 --> 00:09:25,300
Judge models make things too, so
these go to a person for review

154
00:09:25,364 --> 00:09:26,696
instead of blocking automatically.

155
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:30,010
But I track them, and I watch the trend.

156
00:09:30,769 --> 00:09:35,832
Third is about behavior, meaning what
the agent did and not only what it said.

157
00:09:37,076 --> 00:09:41,038
Did it call a tool with a service cr-
credential instead of its user's own?

158
00:09:41,457 --> 00:09:41,696
Right?

159
00:09:41,696 --> 00:09:45,297
So did it ask for records or
fields outside the expected set?

160
00:09:45,676 --> 00:09:48,435
Did it write restricted
text to a shared r- record?

161
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:52,342
So on the right is roughly what
a test case looks like, right?

162
00:09:52,495 --> 00:09:57,454
A task, a persona, the canaries it
can't contain, the facts it can't

163
00:09:57,467 --> 00:10:02,301
h- hint at, or rules for its tools
calls, and what happens if it fails.

164
00:10:02,874 --> 00:10:05,814
One line I would point
out is check every turn.

165
00:10:06,329 --> 00:10:11,455
In a multi-turn conversation, run
the checks after each turn because

166
00:10:11,465 --> 00:10:15,497
something can leak in turn three and,
and never show up in the final answer

167
00:10:16,497 --> 00:10:17,603
So combination leaks.

168
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:19,058
Now the hard one, right?

169
00:10:19,059 --> 00:10:21,693
So each fact on its own, is, is allowed.

170
00:10:22,238 --> 00:10:23,797
Putting them together isn't.

171
00:10:24,339 --> 00:10:27,884
In my research, I've been calling
this permission aggregation

172
00:10:27,892 --> 00:10:32,827
disclosure, which is a long name for
a leak that's built entirely out of

173
00:10:32,861 --> 00:10:34,334
things the user was allowed to see.

174
00:10:35,521 --> 00:10:36,367
Here's an example

175
00:10:37,367 --> 00:10:40,956
Our coverage banker can see
a number on a dashboard.

176
00:10:42,476 --> 00:10:46,569
One, live sell-side mandate in
industrials in the Northeast.

177
00:10:47,107 --> 00:10:47,622
That's fine.

178
00:10:47,622 --> 00:10:48,585
It's just account.

179
00:10:49,706 --> 00:10:53,862
They can also see the activity feed
for accounts in their sector, and

180
00:10:53,862 --> 00:10:57,364
it shows that the M&A coverage must,
a banker, It shows that the M&A

181
00:10:57,385 --> 00:11:02,815
coverage banker met Northwind CFO
three times this month Also fine.

182
00:11:03,428 --> 00:11:05,139
it's only the subject
line, no details, right?

183
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:08,244
So now they ask the agent
what's going on with Northwind.

184
00:11:09,418 --> 00:11:14,262
A helpful agent connects the two and says
Northwind is probably exploring a sale.

185
00:11:14,903 --> 00:11:17,571
That's material non-public
information, and it's made

186
00:11:17,571 --> 00:11:19,049
entirely out of allowed pieces.

187
00:11:19,514 --> 00:11:23,420
None of the source systems did anything
wrong, and there's no canary in, in that

188
00:11:23,421 --> 00:11:25,297
sentence for an exact match to catch.

189
00:11:26,258 --> 00:11:30,149
This is what I meant earlier about better
models making the-- making this harder.

190
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:32,165
So how do you test it?

191
00:11:33,155 --> 00:11:36,901
I build scenarios on purpose where
joining allowed results narrows

192
00:11:36,923 --> 00:11:38,405
things down to a single record.

193
00:11:39,627 --> 00:11:42,552
Then I check that the agent
doesn't ta- take that last step.

194
00:11:43,255 --> 00:11:47,127
It shouldn't name the company,
rank how likely it is or speculate.

195
00:11:47,684 --> 00:11:49,125
I would start with three of these, right?

196
00:11:49,136 --> 00:11:52,411
So you probably learn more from
them than, a hundred record--

197
00:11:52,485 --> 00:11:54,221
a hundred single record tests

198
00:11:55,221 --> 00:11:57,587
So when do you run, all of this?

199
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:00,021
Not only when the code changes.

200
00:12:00,834 --> 00:12:04,731
With agents, behavior also changes
when things around the code change.

201
00:12:06,127 --> 00:12:07,538
A model upgrade is a big one.

202
00:12:08,498 --> 00:12:12,345
A new model comes with new habits and
is often more willing to summarize

203
00:12:12,345 --> 00:12:13,748
and infer, so I run everything.

204
00:12:14,767 --> 00:12:18,944
A change to a system prompt or a
policy shift, or, or a policy shifts

205
00:12:18,953 --> 00:12:20,826
how the agent refuses and summarizes.

206
00:12:22,050 --> 00:12:26,893
A new tool or MCP server is a new path
to, to data with its own credential,

207
00:12:27,127 --> 00:12:30,410
and in my experience, that's the most
common way a leak gets introduced.

208
00:12:31,463 --> 00:12:34,796
A schema change on a tool can
quietly add fields to a payload.

209
00:12:35,665 --> 00:12:38,911
A change to permissions or sharing
rules in the source system moves

210
00:12:38,912 --> 00:12:42,443
the expected sets, so I rebuild
those and run everything again.

211
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:46,721
And a rebuild of the retrieval
index or memory is exactly where

212
00:12:46,725 --> 00:12:48,517
ACLs tend to get lost in chunking.

213
00:12:49,887 --> 00:12:51,434
For gating, I keep it simple.

214
00:12:52,316 --> 00:12:55,620
An exact canary match blocks
the release, and the only

215
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:56,885
acceptable number there is zero.

216
00:12:57,889 --> 00:12:59,368
A wrong credential blocks it too.

217
00:13:00,154 --> 00:13:03,885
Meaning base hits go on-- go to
a named reviewer with a deadline,

218
00:13:04,190 --> 00:13:05,511
and we keep an eye on the trend.

219
00:13:06,708 --> 00:13:10,112
If a failure here can't stop a
release, then it isn't really

220
00:13:10,117 --> 00:13:11,344
part of your pipeline yet

221
00:13:12,344 --> 00:13:16,221
A test that just says failed
doesn't help much, at 6:00 in

222
00:13:16,222 --> 00:13:17,469
the evening before a release.

223
00:13:18,082 --> 00:13:19,835
You need to know where the leak came from.

224
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:23,285
So the harness keeps a full
trace and walks it backwards.

225
00:13:23,906 --> 00:13:27,556
Start with the sentence that leaked,
find the bit of context the model took

226
00:13:27,556 --> 00:13:32,640
it from, find the tool call that loaded
that context, say call number four.

227
00:13:33,473 --> 00:13:37,944
From that call, get the credential it
ran as and the record IDs it got back.

228
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:41,308
And from those, find the rule
that should have stopped it.

229
00:13:42,497 --> 00:13:46,497
Once you do this a few times, the failures
tend to fall into a handful of causes,

230
00:13:46,892 --> 00:13:50,371
and each one gets fixed in a different
phase, usually by a different team.

231
00:13:51,276 --> 00:13:54,930
Overfetching gets fixed in the
tool's contract, so it only returns

232
00:13:54,932 --> 00:13:56,526
what it-- what the task, needs.

233
00:13:57,244 --> 00:14:00,582
A service credential gets
fixed in identity by passing

234
00:14:00,584 --> 00:14:01,639
the user's own token through.

235
00:14:02,954 --> 00:14:05,785
Too much being passed between
agents gets fixed in orchestration.

236
00:14:06,977 --> 00:14:09,586
Memory or cache problems gets
fixed in how the store is

237
00:14:09,586 --> 00:14:10,982
access-- how the store is scoped.

238
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:16,324
And the last one, inference from
data that was all allowed, gets

239
00:14:16,325 --> 00:14:17,701
fixed in policy and prompts.

240
00:14:18,748 --> 00:14:22,081
That one also needs a person to decide
what level of risk is acceptable

241
00:14:22,105 --> 00:14:23,854
because a test can't decide that for you

242
00:14:24,854 --> 00:14:26,498
So where does this sit in the pipeline?

243
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:30,688
On every pull request, I
ru- I run tool-level tests.

244
00:14:30,965 --> 00:14:35,761
Each tool called as each persona should
return what the expected set says.

245
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:40,090
They are f- they are fast, and they catch
most over-fetching and credential problems

246
00:14:40,303 --> 00:14:44,520
before a model is even allowed or if a, if
a, before a model is even, even involved.

247
00:14:45,211 --> 00:14:46,180
Then the persona suite.

248
00:14:46,814 --> 00:14:49,213
Full agent runs for every
persona and every task.

249
00:14:49,812 --> 00:14:53,607
That runs, that runs on any of the changes
from the previous, slide in nightly.

250
00:14:54,732 --> 00:14:58,758
Before a release, the joint
scenarios, those are slower and

251
00:14:58,758 --> 00:14:59,780
need someone to review them.

252
00:15:00,648 --> 00:15:04,860
Then the gate, which blocks the promotion
on exact matches and wrong credentials.

253
00:15:05,725 --> 00:15:10,628
After release, I keep a few canaries
in production as tripwires, records

254
00:15:10,628 --> 00:15:12,904
that no real user should ever get back.

255
00:15:13,286 --> 00:15:15,731
If one shows up in an output,
we know straight away.

256
00:15:16,677 --> 00:15:19,014
We also, sample real
traces and audit them.

257
00:15:20,085 --> 00:15:24,138
For an MLOps audience, the easiest
way to think about it, treat this

258
00:15:24,139 --> 00:15:26,311
like any other offline evaluation.

259
00:15:26,634 --> 00:15:29,658
It runs when a model version gets
promoted in your registry and

260
00:15:29,659 --> 00:15:30,895
a failure stops the promotion.

261
00:15:31,697 --> 00:15:34,135
One thing I would avoid
is gating on a pass rate.

262
00:15:35,436 --> 00:15:39,202
A ninety-nine percent pass
rate here means, one answer

263
00:15:39,202 --> 00:15:40,449
in a hundred leaked something.

264
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:44,003
If an evaluation doesn't blo-
block a deploy, it doesn't

265
00:15:44,006 --> 00:15:45,128
change what gets shipped

266
00:15:46,128 --> 00:15:49,177
A few things, from the CRM
side of my career, because

267
00:15:49,191 --> 00:15:50,333
honestly, none of this is new.

268
00:15:51,215 --> 00:15:53,237
Agents just raise the stakes.

269
00:15:54,690 --> 00:15:57,331
First, enforce the rules
where the data lives.

270
00:15:57,904 --> 00:16:01,269
On a large service platform I worked
on with more than ten thousand

271
00:16:01,269 --> 00:16:04,855
users, the CRM mostly pulled
together data from legacy systems

272
00:16:05,146 --> 00:16:06,429
that were the real source of truth.

273
00:16:07,052 --> 00:16:10,290
The rule there was that sharing
logic stays in the system of record,

274
00:16:10,871 --> 00:16:12,614
and the layer on top inherits it.

275
00:16:13,268 --> 00:16:14,963
I think agents should work the same way.

276
00:16:15,611 --> 00:16:19,365
Second, don't hide an admin
account behind a chat box.

277
00:16:19,699 --> 00:16:22,255
The quickest way to build an
agent is to give it an integration

278
00:16:22,255 --> 00:16:23,408
user that can see everything.

279
00:16:24,110 --> 00:16:27,281
But then every person who talks to
it effectively has admin access.

280
00:16:28,283 --> 00:16:30,625
Pass the user's own
identity through instead.

281
00:16:31,772 --> 00:16:34,065
Third, look after your test users.

282
00:16:34,810 --> 00:16:37,852
I have built access control frameworks
for programmers, programs re- regulated

283
00:16:37,852 --> 00:16:41,509
in s- several countries at once,
and access there never stays still.

284
00:16:42,146 --> 00:16:46,473
Regulation changes, the org chart
changes, so the personas need versions,

285
00:16:46,888 --> 00:16:49,294
reviews, and an owner, and same as code.

286
00:16:50,002 --> 00:16:53,140
Fourth, check every place
where data gets merged.

287
00:16:53,689 --> 00:16:58,218
In my experience, that's where a source
system's rules quietly get dropped, and an

288
00:16:58,219 --> 00:17:00,656
agent merges data on every single request

289
00:17:01,656 --> 00:17:04,463
So if you want to start on this
next week, here's roughly the

290
00:17:04,464 --> 00:17:05,783
order I would go in, right?

291
00:17:06,077 --> 00:17:09,682
Set up four to six test users with
real permissions in a test environment.

292
00:17:10,236 --> 00:17:12,828
Plan canaries in records,
notes, files, and email.

293
00:17:13,533 --> 00:17:16,991
Build the expected sets by querying
as, as each of those users.

294
00:17:17,485 --> 00:17:21,257
Write the three kinds of checks:
exact match, meaning, and behavior.

295
00:17:22,571 --> 00:17:25,533
And three join scenarios that
narrow down to a single record.

296
00:17:26,028 --> 00:17:28,967
And run the whole thing whenever the
model, prompt, tools or permissions

297
00:17:28,967 --> 00:17:31,239
change with the power to block a release.

298
00:17:32,241 --> 00:17:35,984
Then when something fails, make
sure the failure points to the tool

299
00:17:36,005 --> 00:17:39,425
call and context that caused it, so
the right team can get the ticket.

300
00:17:40,535 --> 00:17:42,423
You don't need a new
platform for any of this.

301
00:17:42,742 --> 00:17:46,746
It's test data, test users, a trace,
and a team agreeing that authorization

302
00:17:46,746 --> 00:17:48,542
is part of what ready to release means

303
00:17:49,542 --> 00:17:50,265
That's what I had.

304
00:17:50,471 --> 00:17:54,099
the way I would sum it up, accuracy
tells you the agent is smart and

305
00:17:54,099 --> 00:17:58,127
authorization evaluates-- authorization
evals tell you whether it's safe

306
00:17:58,128 --> 00:17:59,649
to put in front of your users.

307
00:18:00,283 --> 00:18:02,991
In a regulated business, you
need both before you ship.

308
00:18:03,779 --> 00:18:05,980
If you want the longer version
of the argument, the paper

309
00:18:05,987 --> 00:18:07,292
behind this talk is, linked here.

310
00:18:07,292 --> 00:18:08,143
Thanks for watching.

311
00:18:08,177 --> 00:18:10,417
If you have any questions,
find me in the Conf42

312
00:18:10,417 --> 00:18:11,768
community chat or on LinkedIn.

313
00:18:12,497 --> 00:18:15,107
I would really like to hear how
other teams are testing this
